  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "If $k(1-{7}^{10})$ is the sum of the first $10$ terms of the geometric series $-1+7-49+\u22ef,$ then $k=$",
  "worked_solution": "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nIn our case, ${a}_{1}=-1.$ To find the value of $r,$ we find the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{7}{-1}=-7\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=-1,N=10$ and $r=-7$ in the given formula, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{10} & =-1\\cdot \\frac{1-(-7)^{10}}{1-(-7)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-{7}^{10}}{1+7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-{7}^{10}}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(1-{7}^{10})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the above with $k(1-{7}^{10}),$ we obtain $k=-\\frac{1}{8}.$",
